sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2015-girardia-13> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In G. dorotocephala, the long-lasting alteration of bioelectric patterning after brief octanol exposure is not caused by residual octanol (which leaves tissues within 24 h) but is initiated by octanol's disruption of gap junction communication, which alters bioelectric network topology and results in a persistent perturbation of bioelectric state.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Brief gap-junction blockade persistently alters bioelectric state after drug washout";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Emmons-Bell M, Durant F, Hammelman J, Bessonov N, Volpert V, Morokuma J, Pinet K, Adams DS, Pietak A, Lobo D, Levin M, “Gap Junctional Blockade Stochastically Induces Different Species-Specific Head Anatomies in Genetically Wild-Type Girardia dorotocephala Flatworms”, International journal of molecular sciences (2015), doi:10.3390/ijms161126065; section “3. Discussion › 3.4. Bioelectric State of Tissues and Its Stable Alteration in Species-Specific Shape Change”. Scope: G. dorotocephala somatic tissues after transient octanol exposure"
}
